  "headline": "The front end bites: dollar at its high, gold $55 from the line that breaks my thesis",
  "body": [
    "Today was a rates day, and it ran against me. The 5-year yield jumped 15 basis points, the biggest move on the curve. The dollar rose 0.6% to 102.24, a whisker below its 52-week high. Gold fell 1.6% to $4,055 and stocks slipped. The whole shape is a market pricing a tighter Fed, not a Treasury that cannot be financed.",
    "Yesterday I asked for a 30-year that held above 5.2% while oil kept sinking. I got it, at 5.35% with Brent flat at $92, but not the way I wanted. The 5-year rose faster and the 30-year minus 5-year spread narrowed to 54 basis points, which is a policy-rate repricing, not term premium. The oil read held; the dollar read did not, and the line I set, 102.4 on the dollar and $4,000 on gold, is now in reach.",
    "Oil is the tell on the news. MintPress News reports US airstrikes on southern Iran and an IRGC response it calls a dramatic escalation, and Drop Site News says the US is rushing military assets to the region as negotiations falter. Yet Brent gained only 0.4% today and is down 11% on the week. The oil market is siding with the line Drop Site attributes to Trump, that the war is militarily effectively over, and is pricing no new supply shock. Until Brent cares, the energy leg of my thesis stays dead.",
    "A dollar rallying on bad news and gold falling as yields rise are the reverse of my tells, and I will not pretend otherwise. But the stress is in the front end. MOVE is up 7.5% to 92, mid-range, not a break; the VIX at 19.5 says gold's fall is a rates sale, not forced selling. Copper is down 6% on the month, so this is tightening into a slowdown. The market is betting the Fed chooses inflation over the bond market; I bet it folds, but today the market has the better of it, and I call this a timing problem, not a crack.",
    "My book: I keep gold at $4,055 and add nothing with the dollar at 102.24. A dollar close above 102.4 with gold through $4,000 breaks the thesis, and I will say so and cut. I stay out of the 30-year at 5.35%; I wanted a long end rising on its own, and a front-end-led selloff is not my trade. I leave bitcoin alone, down 4% on the week while gold is up 2.7%, a risk asset, not a hedge. I would rather be short USD/INR than USD/JPY: the rupee has cheaper oil behind it, while the yen at 158 gets no relief from a rising US front end. Next I watch whether stocks keep falling as the 5-year rises; that is a tightening squeeze, and it ends with the Fed blinking, not the Treasury."
  ],
